usb: gadget: f_acm: don't wait for host connect, fix two UAFs

acm_stdio_start() looped on `while (!acm_connected(dev))`, calling
ctrlc() on every iteration. ctrlc() polls tstc() on the CURRENT
stdin -- and stdin is already pointed at "usbacm" at this point,
since acm_stdio_start() runs from inside console_assign()/iomux_doenv()
while the device is being assigned. So ctrlc() re-enters
acm_stdio_tstc() -> dm_usb_gadget_handle_interrupts(f_acm->udc),
nested on top of the outer loop's own acm_connected() ->
dm_usb_gadget_handle_interrupts(f_acm->udc) call on the same UDC.
That nested/re-entrant interrupt servicing corrupts ci_udc's
queue-head/endpoint state (observed as "CACHE: Misaligned operation
at range [00000001, ...]" and a data abort a moment later).

DFU's equivalent loop in common/dfu.c (run_usb_dnl_gadget()) also
calls ctrlc(), but never hits this: its stdin stays on plain serial
throughout, so its ctrlc() never touches the gadget it just
registered. That's the actual difference between DFU (works) and
ACM (crashed) -- not a missing init call, both use the identical
udc_device_get_by_index() / g_dnl_clear_detach() / g_dnl_register()
sequence.

There's no need to wait here at all. acm_stdio_puts()/putc() already
push into tx_buf before checking f_acm->connected and simply return
without transmitting when not connected -- output is buffered and
flushed by the next __acm_tx() once a host attaches. acm_stdio_tstc()
returns 0 on an empty rx_buf with a single (non-reentrant) interrupt
poll. So returning immediately after g_dnl_register() is sufficient:
the console comes up right away, works normally once/if a host
attaches, and degrades to "output silently buffered" rather than
hanging or crashing when it doesn't.

Second bug, same file: acm_stdio_stop() calls g_dnl_unregister(),
which tears down the composite and frees the f_acm struct (via
acm_unbind()) -- but it never clears the stdio_dev's dev->priv,
which still points at that now-freed f_acm. acm_stdio_start() checks
`if (dev->priv) return 0;` as its "already registered" fast path, so
after any stop/start cycle it early-returns without calling
g_dnl_register() again, leaving dev->priv dangling. The next
putc()/tstc() on "usbacm" dereferences the freed f_acm via
stdio_to_acm(dev) -- a use-after-free, seen on hardware as an
"undefined instruction" fault jumping into freed heap.

This stop/start cycle isn't hypothetical: adi_loadvals unconditionally
reverts the console to serial-only right before its saveenv, which
drops "usbacm" from the console set and calls acm_stdio_stop(); the
next preboot's `env import` from uEnv.txt (which carries the
"serial@e0001000,usbacm" default) re-adds it and calls
acm_stdio_start() straight into the dangling pointer. Clearing
dev->priv in acm_stdio_stop() makes the next start() re-register
properly instead of resurrecting a freed pointer.

Third bug, same file: drv_usbacm_init() builds its struct stdio_dev
on the stack and never initializes .flush, then passes it to
stdio_register() -> stdio_register_dev() -> stdio_clone(), which
calloc()s a fresh copy and then memcpy()s the whole struct on top,
carrying over whatever garbage happened to be on the stack at
&stdio.flush into the permanently registered device. console_flush()
(enabled by CONFIG_CONSOLE_FLUSH_SUPPORT=y here) later does
`if (dev->flush != NULL) dev->flush(dev);` -- if that garbage byte
pattern is non-NULL, it is called as a function pointer straight
into whatever was left on the stack, producing an "undefined
instruction" fault into an address that looks like heap/stack, not
code. This matches hardware crashes with byte-for-byte identical
register dumps across unrelated boots: the stack layout leading up
to drv_usbacm_init() is itself deterministic given the same boot
path, so the leftover garbage -- and thus the crash -- reproduces
identically until the stack contents change. Zeroing the struct
before use removes the depends-on-stack-garbage behavior entirely.

--- a/drivers/usb/gadget/f_acm.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/gadget/f_acm.c
@@ -646,12 +646,12 @@
 	else
 		return -ENODEV;
 
-	while (!acm_connected(dev)) {
-		if (ctrlc())
-			return -ECANCELED;
-
-		schedule();
-	}
+	/*
+	 * Do not wait for a host here: ctrlc() would re-enter this same
+	 * UDC's interrupt handling while stdin is mid-assignment to this
+	 * device, corrupting ci_udc state. putc()/puts()/tstc() already
+	 * tolerate no host being attached yet.
+	 */
 
 	return 0;
 }
@@ -659,7 +659,8 @@
 static int acm_stdio_stop(struct stdio_dev *dev)
 {
 	g_dnl_unregister();
+	dev->priv = NULL;
 	g_dnl_clear_detach();
 
 	return 0;
 }
@@ -667,6 +668,7 @@
 int drv_usbacm_init(void)
 {
 	struct stdio_dev stdio;
 
+	memset(&stdio, 0, sizeof(stdio));
 	strcpy(stdio.name, "usbacm");
 	stdio.flags = DEV_FLAGS_INPUT | DEV_FLAGS_OUTPUT;
